<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bletchley Park</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bletchley Park is a country house and estate in Buckinghamshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, that was the centre of British codebreaking during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Thousands of staff, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-turing">Alan Turing</a>, broke German Enigma and Lorenz ciphers there, producing the intelligence known as Ultra, and the work led to Colossus, one of the first programmable electronic computers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bletchley-park.jpg" alt="Bletchley Park" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DeFacto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bletchley_Park_Mansion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bletchley Park lies in Bletchley, Milton Keynes. The estate took its name after the architect Samuel Lipscomb Seckham bought it in 1877 and built a house there. In 1938 a builder bought the mansion for a housing estate, but in May of that year Admiral Sir Hugh Sinclair, head of the Secret Intelligence Service, bought it with 58 acres for use in a war, using £6,000 of his own money because the government said it had no budget. The site was close to Bletchley railway station, where the line between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> met the main west coast line, and to good <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> links.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first staff of the Government Code and Cypher School moved to the Park on 15 August 1939. Five weeks before the war began, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>'s Cipher Bureau had revealed its success against Enigma to British and French personnel, and the British used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>' techniques. Wooden huts were built from late 1939, and a neighbouring school was taken over.</p><p>The recruits included linguists, chess players and papyrologists, and in 1941 The Daily Telegraph was asked to run a crossword competition so that promising solvers could be approached discreetly. The staff were about 75 per cent women. All signed the Official Secrets Act, and the intelligence was kept as the Ultra secret.</p><p>The bombe, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alan-turing">Alan Turing</a> with an important contribution from Gordon Welchman and built by Harold Keen of the British Tabulating Machine Company, found daily Enigma settings. Hut 3 turned decrypts into intelligence reports, and the more complex Lorenz cipher, called Tunny, was attacked with machinery that led to Colossus. At its peak about 4,000 messages a day were being read.</p><p>In October 1941 Turing, Welchman, Hugh Alexander and Stuart Milner-Barry wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> about staff shortages, and he ordered action that day. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> joined the war American cryptographers were posted to Hut 3, and the BRUSA Agreement of 1943 foreshadowed the Five Eyes partnership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Government Code and Cypher School became GCHQ, which moved to Eastcote in 1946 and Cheltenham in 1951. Because of the thirty year rule there was no official mention of the work until the mid-1970s, and F. W. Winterbotham's The Ultra Secret appeared in 1974. The Bletchley Park Trust was set up in 1991, and the site opened to visitors in 1993. It houses the Bletchley Park museum and, in Block H, the independent National Museum of Computing, which has a working reconstruction of a bombe and a rebuilt Colossus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bletchley Park” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its resistance to Nazi Germany in 1940–1941, when it was the only major power still fighting, was decisive for the outcome of the Second World War, and the codebreakers of <b>Bletchley Park</b>, among them Alan Turing, contributed to Allied victory and to the birth of computing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
